---
// /prototypes/setup/project-stages: the Project stages milestone, as a walk.
//
// Answers to docs/product-brief.md § 5, for this screen:
//   WHO         Dana, the tenant admin, standing the program up alone or beside
//               the account team.
//   DOING WHAT  Saying which of ProjectFirma's six stages her program's projects
//               actually move through, and which one a new project starts in.
//   BRINGING    A project tracker whose Status column holds the program's own
//               words for the same ladder (Proposed, Funded, In construction,
//               Monitoring, Complete).
//   OPTIMIZING  Confirming the match between her statuses and the six over
//               learning the six from scratch; one question a screen; nothing
//               lost between sittings.
//   PAYS FOR    Two program preferences: the stages in use (a tenant-side subset
//               of the global dbo.ProjectStage rows) and the stage a new
//               project's ProjectStageID starts at.
//   LEAVES OUT  Proposal approval and the public visibility of pending
//               proposals. See below.
//
// THE STAGES ARE NOT CONFIGURATION. The teammate's 2026-09-23 "Project Stages
// Setup" mock made the case: dbo.ProjectStage is six global rows (Proposal,
// Planning & Design, Implementation, Post-Implementation, Completed, Deferred)
// with no TenantID, not aliasable, and "project stage" is an attribute of a
// project, not something a tenant builds. There is nothing to extract and
// nothing to author. What a program does have is PREFERENCE: which of the six
// it uses (her example, a program that skips proposals) and where a new project
// starts. The step stays in setup because the documents already carry evidence
// for the first answer: the tracker's Status values are extracted as lifecycle
// candidates, and the walk maps them onto the fixed six so the program confirms
// a match instead of answering cold.
//
// THREE SCREENS, IN THE FUNDING WALK'S FORMAT. The six as tiles, one question
// over the whole cast, pre-pressed where a tracker status lands; the starting
// stage, one choice over the stages in use; the roster and "Go to setup".
// The shell is firma2-setup-stepper and the screens are the three components
// below. The walk asks subset first and default second, the reverse of the
// mock's order, because the default can only name a stage the program uses.
//
// NEITHER ANSWER HAS A COLUMN YET. Her mock's developer notes are the record:
// no table lets a tenant turn a stage off (the platform's shape for it would be
// a tenant table pointing at the global one, as dbo.TenantFeatureFlag points at
// dbo.FeatureFlag), and Project.ProjectStageID is NOT NULL with no DEFAULT and
// no create-project endpoint to read one. The walk captures both so whoever
// builds project creation does not have to ask.
//
// APPROVAL AND VISIBILITY ARE CUT (agreed with the teammate, who flagged them
// as out of onboarding scope herself). Her mock asked whether a proposal needs
// approval before it counts, and whether a pending one is Public or Protected.
// dbo.Project has no approval or visibility column, and Public would need an
// anonymous read surface the app does not have. ProjectFirma 1.0 had the
// feature; rebuilding it is product work, not a setup answer.
import SetupLayout from '../../../layouts/SetupLayout.astro';
import Firma2SetupStepper from '../../../components/setup/firma2-setup-stepper.astro';
import type { SetupStep } from '../../../lib/setup-steps';
import Firma2SetupStagePicker from '../../../components/setup/firma2-setup-stage-picker.astro';
import Firma2SetupStageDefault from '../../../components/setup/firma2-setup-stage-default.astro';
import Firma2SetupStageRoster from '../../../components/setup/firma2-setup-stage-roster.astro';
import EsaBadge from '@esa/ecology/esa-badge.astro';

// The walk and its rail labels (firma2-setup-step-rail); each Next names the step after it.
const steps: SetupStep[] = [
  { id: 'suggested', label: 'Stages in use' },
  { id: 'default', label: 'Starting stage' },
  { id: 'review', label: 'Review' },
];
const stepIds = steps.map((step) => step.id);
---
<!-- manifest:
  layout: stack(2xl)
  sections:
    - status counts  -> esa-badge   (two, slotted into the milestone band SetupLayout renders)
    - walk           -> firma2-setup-stepper   (rail of labelled steps: firma2-setup-step-rail)
      - suggested      -> firma2-setup-stage-picker
      - default        -> firma2-setup-stage-default
      - review         -> firma2-setup-stage-roster
-->
<SetupLayout title="Project stages" width="wide" milestone="lifecycle">
  <!-- The counts the screens produce, at the top of the page. Synced by the
       script below from the same draft the screens read. -->
  <span slot="status" data-part="used-count"><EsaBadge value="0 in use" variant="secondary" size="sm" /></span>
  <span slot="status" data-part="review-count"><EsaBadge value="6 to review" variant="secondary" size="sm" /></span>

  <Firma2SetupStepper steps={steps} stepIds={stepIds}>
    <Firma2SetupStagePicker />
    <Firma2SetupStageDefault />
    <Firma2SetupStageRoster />
  </Firma2SetupStepper>
</SetupLayout>

<script>
  import { listStages } from '../../../lib/setup-draft';

  const usedBadge = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-part="used-count"] .esa-badge__text');
  const reviewBadge = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-part="review-count"] .esa-badge__text');

  const sync = (): void => {
    const stages = listStages();
    const used = stages.filter((stage) => stage.status === 'confirmed').length;
    const toReview = stages.filter((stage) => stage.status === 'suggested').length;

    if (usedBadge) usedBadge.textContent = `${used} in use`;
    if (reviewBadge) reviewBadge.textContent = `${toReview} to review`;
  };

  document.addEventListener('setup-draft-change', sync);
  sync();
</script>
